NEEDS TONY -- RULED 2026-11-03 (Tony, via Clay), except item 3
    1. RULED: style-setting attributes may go directly on fields.
    2. RULED: style changes are scoped to the field's subtree. Any field can
       override by naming its own style or change.
    3. RULED 2026-11-04 (forms plan, "Event firing"); the question as it stood: event-time firing. setAction is gone. How
       does kant fire an attribute's action at event time -- what finds it, in
       what order, and what decides that a click or key belongs to it? (Old
       GUI bubbled from the clicked field up through its parents.)
    4. RULED: the drop list (foot of this file) is confirmed as listed. If
       something dropped is wanted later, it comes back then.
    5. RULED: pad is parked until A4. top / bottom / left / right are border
       sides (Bot drew them per side), kept, spelling settled at A4. fill is
       NOT inherited. fit=text is dropped.
    PAD DROPPED 2026-11-04 (Tony, SEQ 532): item 5's "parked" is superseded.
    onLayout DROPPED 2026-11-04 (Tony). Click actions use onClick, an attribute naming the action.
    SIDES RULED 2026-11-04 (Tony): topBorder bottomBorder leftBorder rightBorder; bare = width 1, =number = width;
    naming any side draws only the named sides; plain border stays four-sided.
    The rows below still carry their pre-ruling leans; where a ruling moved
    one, the row says "ruled".

COLUMNS
    moment      define / layout / first layout / event  (two may apply)
    inh         inherited: yes = found by walking the parents; no = own only
    act         has action: yes = it hangs (or is) an action fired at event
                time
    depends on  what it leans on. LISTENER marks the listener macro --
                bound to a source or the selection and re-run when it
                changes. That is A2's question; every LISTENER row waits on it.
    C++ floor   the native primitive it finally needs, or "none" (pure kant)
    lean        keep / respell / drop / needs Tony; drop and respell rows
                whose old behaviour did not match their name carry a reason.

